  - mcp tools
  - mcp client
  - server tools
  - createMCPClient
  - createMCPClients
  - type safety
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" is a host-side "},{"type":"link","href":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model Context Protocol"}]},{"type":"text","value":" client for TanStack AI. It connects your server route to any MCP-compliant server and makes that server's tools, resources, and prompts available inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP tool execution is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server-side only"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" call lives in a server route (or serverless function) — never in browser code."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-mcp-and-webmcp","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server MCP and WebMCP"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server MCP and WebMCP solve different problems."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Integration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where it runs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What it does"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server MCP"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your server route"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Connects TanStack "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" to tools, resources, and prompts from an MCP server."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The browser page"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exposes executable client tools to a browser agent through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"document.modelContext"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP calls return directly to the browser agent. They do not become tool results in a TanStack chat run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./webmcp","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to expose browser actions. If your server needs MCP server tools, continue with this guide."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"package-manager","mode":"install"},"children":[],"properties":{"data-package-manager-meta":"{\"packagesByFramework\":{\"react\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]],\"vue\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]],\"solid\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]],\"svelte\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]],\"preact\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]],\"angular\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]],\"vanilla\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]],\"octane\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-mcp\",\"@modelcontextprotocol/client\"]]},\"mode\":\"install\"}"}},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" tries protocol "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2026-07-28"},{"type":"text","value":" first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package names for an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk"},{"type":"text","value":" import are in "},{"type":"link","href":"../migration/mcp-sdk","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP SDK packages"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"quick-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest integration is the managed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" option: hand the client to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" and it discovers the tools and closes the connection when the run ends — no lifecycle code at all."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/routes/api.chat.ts  (TanStack Start)\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        const mcp = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: {\n            type: 'http',\n            url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n          },\n        })\n\n        // chat() discovers the tools and closes the client when the run ends.\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: { clients: [mcp] },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Need fully-typed tool arguments, resources, prompts, or your own lifecycle? Spread tools manually instead — see "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-manual","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"link","href":"#lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifecycle"}]},{"type":"text","value":" section below."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the client side, consume the stream with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" exactly as you would any other TanStack AI endpoint:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/components/Chat.tsx\nimport { useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, status } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => (\n        <div key={m.id}>\n          <strong>{m.role}:</strong>{' '}\n          {m.parts.find((p) => p.type === 'text')?.content}\n        </div>\n      ))}\n      <button\n        onClick={() => sendMessage({ content: 'Hello' })}\n        disabled={status === 'streaming'}\n      >\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"transports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transports"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"http-streamable-http","children":[{"type":"text","value":"HTTP (Streamable HTTP)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The preferred transport for remote servers. Uses the MCP Streamable HTTP protocol."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sse-server-sent-events","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SSE (Server-Sent Events)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For servers that implement the legacy SSE transport."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'sse',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/sse',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"stdio-node-js-only","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stdio (Node.js only)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For spawning a local MCP process. Because stdio imports Node-native modules, it is isolated behind a subpath import so edge bundles stay clean."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { stdioTransport } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp/stdio'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: stdioTransport({\n    command: 'node',\n    args: ['./my-mcp-server.js'],\n    env: { API_KEY: process.env.API_KEY ?? '' },\n  }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"custom-transport-escape-hatch","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom transport (escape hatch)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass any "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transport"},{"type":"text","value":" instance directly as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transport"},{"type":"text","value":" option. For in-process testing, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InMemoryTransport"},{"type":"text","value":" is re-exported from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient, InMemoryTransport } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst [clientTransport, serverTransport] = InMemoryTransport.createLinkedPair()\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: clientTransport })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a custom network transport, pass a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Transport"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@modelcontextprotocol/client"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/client'\n\nconst transport = new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(new URL('https://example.com/mcp'))\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport })","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"authentication","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authentication"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"static-tokens-headers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static tokens (headers)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For servers that take a pre-provisioned API key or bearer token, pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":" on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"http"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sse"},{"type":"text","value":" transport config — they are sent with every request:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"oauth-authprovider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OAuth ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authProvider"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the server implements the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/authorization","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP authorization spec"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (OAuth 2.1), pass an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"http"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sse"},{"type":"text","value":" config."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" value is an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"OAuthClientProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@modelcontextprotocol/client"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The transport attaches the token. The transport refreshes the token. The transport retries the request on 401."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\nimport type { OAuthClientProvider } from '@modelcontextprotocol/client'\nimport { myTokenStore } from './token-store'\n\n// Server-side: back the provider with tokens you persist (database, KV, ...).\n// `tokens()` returning a valid (or refreshable) token set is all the SDK\n// needs to authenticate requests.\nconst myOAuthProvider: OAuthClientProvider = myTokenStore.provider()\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    authProvider: myOAuthProvider,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactive authorization (redirect flows)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Completing an authorization-code\ngrant requires calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finishAuth(code)"},{"type":"text","value":" on the transport after the user is\nredirected back — and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" constructs the transport internally,\nso it cannot expose it. If you need the interactive flow, build the transport\nyourself and pass it in (the escape hatch above): construct a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamableHTTPClientTransport"},{"type":"text","value":" with your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authProvider"},{"type":"text","value":", keep a reference,\ncall "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transport.finishAuth(code)"},{"type":"text","value":" in your OAuth callback route, then hand the\ntransport to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient({ transport })"},{"type":"text","value":". For typical server-side use —\na provider backed by pre-provisioned or stored tokens with working refresh —\nthe config form shown above is all you need."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"three-modes-of-type-safety","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three Modes of Type Safety"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"mode-1-auto-discovery-client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mode 1 — Auto-discovery ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client.tools()"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools()"},{"type":"text","value":" with no arguments to discover every tool the server exposes. This requires no extra setup. Tool argument types are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" at compile time; the MCP JSON Schema is used for runtime validation."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools()\n// tools: ServerTool[]  — args typed unknown at compile time","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP schemas often use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$ref"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"oneOf"},{"type":"text","value":". On adapters built on the OpenAI API, those tools are sent without strict mode, and the adapter warns in development. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../adapters/openai.md#tools-that-cannot-use-strict-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools that cannot use strict mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Task-based tools are supported."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Tools that declare\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execution.taskSupport: 'required'"},{"type":"text","value":" automatically run through the MCP SDK's\nexperimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tasks/callToolStream"},{"type":"text","value":" flow. TanStack AI waits through task\nstatus updates and returns the terminal result to the model. Tools declaring\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"taskSupport: 'optional'"},{"type":"text","value":" continue to use ordinary "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"callTool"},{"type":"text","value":" execution.\nTask execution needs the server to declare the tasks capability for\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools/call"},{"type":"text","value":"; a server that lists a task-required tool without it is\nskipped by auto-discovery (the tool could never be invoked)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the chat run aborts, TanStack AI stops waiting for the task and sends a\nbest-effort "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tasks/cancel"},{"type":"text","value":" for a remote task the server has already created."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"mode-2-explicit-definitions-client-tools-defs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mode 2 — Explicit definitions ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client.tools([...defs])"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass TanStack "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition()"},{"type":"text","value":" instances to get full TypeScript types and Zod validation. Only the named tools are returned (allowlist). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPToolNotFoundError"},{"type":"text","value":" is thrown if a name isn't on the server. Task-required tools use the same automatic task execution described in Mode 1."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst searchDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'search',\n  description: 'Search for items',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ id: z.string(), title: z.string() })),\n})\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools([searchDef])\n// tools[0].execute is typed: (args: { query: string }) => ...","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"mode-3-generated-types-createmcpclientgeneratedserver","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mode 3 — Generated types ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient<GeneratedServer>"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run the CLI against a live server to generate per-server "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interface"},{"type":"text","value":" types, then pass the generated type as a generic — tool names are narrowed to the server's literal names and pool config keys are compile-checked, with zero runtime overhead. (Tool "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"arguments"}]},{"type":"text","value":" stay untyped on the discovery path — combine with Mode 2 for typed args.)"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-codegen","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Type Generation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp.config.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" setup, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generate"},{"type":"text","value":" CLI, and how to wire the generated types into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-titles-annotations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Titles & Annotations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP servers can ship display and behavior metadata alongside each tool: a human-readable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"title"},{"type":"text","value":" and a set of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"annotations"},{"type":"text","value":" hints ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readOnlyHint"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"destructiveHint"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idempotentHint"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openWorldHint"},{"type":"text","value":", plus a legacy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"annotations.title"},{"type":"text","value":"). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" forwards all of it onto each discovered tool's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.mcp"},{"type":"text","value":", on "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"both"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the auto-discovery and explicit-definition paths, so you can label tools in your UI and decide which ones need a confirmation step."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" field"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"title"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"},{"type":"text","value":" — display name, resolved with the spec's precedence: the tool's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"title"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"annotations.title"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":". Always set."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"annotations"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"annotations"},{"type":"text","value":" object, forwarded verbatim. Absent when the server declares none."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serverToolName"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"},{"type":"text","value":" — server-native (unprefixed) tool name."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serverId"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prefix"},{"type":"text","value":" (undefined when there is none)."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"uiResourceUri"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../mcp/apps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Apps"}]},{"type":"text","value":" widget link, when the tool declares one."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The block is typed, so just read it. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"McpServerTool"},{"type":"text","value":"s — a plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ServerTool"},{"type":"text","value":" (it still drops straight into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ tools })"},{"type":"text","value":") whose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" is statically known to be present and shaped like the table above. No annotation, no cast, and a misspelled field is a compile error:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n\n// Trust comes from YOUR configuration — an allowlist of servers you operate or\n// have vetted — never from anything the server itself sends.\nconst trustedServers = new Set(['https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'])\nconst serverIsTrusted = trustedServers.has(url)\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\n\nconst tools = (await mcp.tools()).map((tool) => {\n  const meta = tool.metadata.mcp\n  const advertisedReadOnly = meta.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true\n  return {\n    ...tool,\n    // Approval is the default. A hint may only relax it for a server whose\n    // trust you established independently; on any other server the same hint\n    // is a label/recommendation and changes nothing about approval.\n    needsApproval: !(serverIsTrusted && advertisedReadOnly),\n  }\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Annotations are advisory, never a security boundary."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The MCP spec is explicit that every field — including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"title"},{"type":"text","value":" — is a hint that may not faithfully describe what the tool actually does, and a malicious or compromised server can claim anything ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readOnlyHint: true"},{"type":"text","value":" on a tool that deletes records). Do not use them as the security boundary for an untrusted server: never let a hint alone waive approval, sandboxing, or authorization. On a server you have independently established as trusted, a hint may "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"relax"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a confirmation step, as above; everywhere else, treat annotations as display labels and recommendations only — surface "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readOnlyHint"},{"type":"text","value":" as a badge (see the UI example below) rather than acting on it."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Titles are display-only: they never change the tool "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" sent to the model, and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prefix"},{"type":"text","value":" still applies to the name ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wx_get_weather"},{"type":"text","value":"), not to the title."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"McpToolMetadata"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"McpServerTool"},{"type":"text","value":" are both exported if you need to name the shapes in your own signatures ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolAnnotations"},{"type":"text","value":" too, re-exported from the MCP SDK). You don't need them just to read the block."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To label tools in your UI, expose the forwarded metadata from a server route — the MCP client itself must stay server-side:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/routes/api.mcp-tools.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/mcp-tools')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async () => {\n        await using mcp = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.MCP_URL! },\n        })\n        const catalog = (await mcp.tools()).map((tool) => ({\n          name: tool.name,\n          // `title` is always set — the fallback chain already ran.\n          title: tool.metadata.mcp.title,\n          description: tool.description,\n          readOnly: tool.metadata.mcp.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n        }))\n        return Response.json({ tools: catalog })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/components/ToolCatalog.tsx\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\n\ninterface ToolSummary {\n  name: string\n  title: string\n  description?: string\n  readOnly: boolean\n}\n\nexport function ToolCatalog() {\n  const [tools, setTools] = useState<Array<ToolSummary>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    fetch('/api/mcp-tools')\n      .then((res) => res.json())\n      .then((body: { tools: Array<ToolSummary> }) => setTools(body.tools))\n  }, [])\n\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {tools.map((tool) => (\n        <li key={tool.name}>\n          {/* Server-declared title, with the hint driving the badge */}\n          <strong>{tool.title}</strong> {tool.readOnly ? '(read-only)' : '(writes)'}\n          <div>{tool.description}</div>\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limit-and-gate-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limit and Gate Tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, the model gets every tool that the server lists, and each tool runs without approval. A server can expose tools that write or delete data. Two client options control this:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolFilter"},{"type":"text","value":": return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" to hide a tool from the model."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":": return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" to pause the run for "},{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"approval"}]},{"type":"text","value":" before each call to the tool."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both options receive the tool definition that the server sent. The definition has the native (unprefixed) "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"title"},{"type":"text","value":", and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"annotations"},{"type":"text","value":". Both options are off by default."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Send only the tools that the server marks read-only:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n})\n\nconst tools = await mcp.tools() // only read-only tools","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A tool with no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"annotations"},{"type":"text","value":" does not pass this filter."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a server that you do not trust, filter by name. You control the names, and the server controls the hints:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst allowed = new Set(['search_issues', 'get_issue'])\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => allowed.has(tool.name),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep every tool, but ask the user before a tool runs:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n// Trust comes from your configuration, not from the server.\nconst trustedServers = new Set(['https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'])\nconst serverIsTrusted = trustedServers.has(url)\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url },\n  // A read-only hint skips approval only on a server that you trust.\n  needsApproval: (tool) =>\n    !(serverIsTrusted && tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run stops with an approval interrupt for each gated call. The client approves or denies it the same way as for any other tool. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval Flow"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a "},{"type":"link","href":"#multi-server-pool","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pool"}]},{"type":"text","value":", each server gets its own options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n    toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n  },\n  docs: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.DOCS_MCP_URL! } },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The options apply in these places:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools()"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" hides tools, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" marks tools."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools([...defs])"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" applies. A definition that the filter hides throws "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPToolNotFoundError"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" does not apply, because each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":" has its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ mcp })"}]},{"type":"text","value":": both apply, because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools()"},{"type":"text","value":" on the client. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-managed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed MCP"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../mcp/apps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Apps"}]},{"type":"text","value":" widget calls"}]},{"type":"text","value":": both apply. A widget cannot call a tool that the filter hides. A widget call has no approval step, so a call to a tool that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" marks returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ ok: false }"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"callTool()"}]},{"type":"text","value":": neither applies. Your code calls the tool directly, and the model is not involved."}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hints come from the server."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A compromised server can mark a delete tool "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readOnlyHint: true"},{"type":"text","value":". Use a name allowlist or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" for a server that you do not trust."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"McpTool"},{"type":"text","value":" is exported if you need to name the type of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool"},{"type":"text","value":" argument."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"multi-server-pool","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Server Pool"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":" connects to many servers in parallel and merges their tools into one flat array. Each server's tools are automatically prefixed with the config key to prevent name collisions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\n// tools: [github_search_repos, github_create_issue, linear_create_issue, ...]\nconst tools = await pool.tools()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pool.tools()"},{"type":"text","value":" collects all servers' tools and throws "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DuplicateToolNameError"},{"type":"text","value":" if any two names collide after prefixing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"per-server-access","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-server access"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\nconst linearTools = await pool.clients.linear!.tools()\nconst resources = await pool.clients.github!.resources()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"disable-or-override-the-prefix","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable or override the prefix"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n    prefix: 'gh',           // override: \"gh_search_repos\"\n  },\n  internal: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.INTERNAL_MCP_URL! },\n    prefix: '',             // disable prefix entirely\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"closing-the-pool","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Closing the pool"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await pool.close()\n// or\nawait using pool = await createMCPClients({ /* server configs */ })","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If any server fails to connect, already-connected clients are closed before the error is thrown — no leaks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifecycle"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can skip this entire section"}]},{"type":"text","value":" by passing clients to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" option (as in the Quick Start) — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" discovers tools and closes the connections when the run ends. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-managed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed MCP with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Read on only if you spread tools manually and own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" yourself."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you manage the client manually, it is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"caller-owned"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" never closes it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools execute "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"lazily while the response stream is consumed"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so only close the client after the stream is fully drained. In a route handler that returns a streaming "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":", a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"try/finally"},{"type":"text","value":" around the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"return"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await using"},{"type":"text","value":" at function scope) closes the client "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"before"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the body streams — in-flight tool calls would fail. Close in a middleware terminal hook instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"streaming-route-handlers-close-via-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming route handlers — close via middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exactly one of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onAbort"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" fires per run, after the agent loop ends:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n  const url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n  const mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: await mcp.tools(),\n    middleware: [\n      {\n        name: 'mcp-close',\n        onFinish: () => mcp.close(),\n        onAbort: () => mcp.close(),\n        onError: () => mcp.close(),\n      },\n    ],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"manual-close-when-you-consume-the-stream-in-scope","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual close — when you consume the stream in scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"try/finally"},{"type":"text","value":" is correct when the stream is drained before the scope exits:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\ntry {\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: await mcp.tools(),\n  })\n  for await (const chunk of stream) {\n    // handle chunks — the stream is fully consumed inside this block\n  }\n} finally {\n  await mcp.close()\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"await-using-explicit-resource-management","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"await using"},{"type":"text","value":" (Explicit Resource Management)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your runtime supports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Symbol.asyncDispose"},{"type":"text","value":" (Node 18.2+ with TypeScript "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target: \"es2022\""},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lib: [\"esnext\"]"},{"type":"text","value":"), the same in-scope-consumption rule applies — the client closes when the block exits:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\nawait using mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools: await mcp.tools(),\n})\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  // handle chunks\n}\n// mcp.close() is called automatically when the block exits","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-name-collisions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Name Collisions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When mixing tools from multiple sources, duplicate names throw "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DuplicateToolNameError"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClients, DuplicateToolNameError } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\ntry {\n  const tools = await pool.tools()\n} catch (err) {\n  if (err instanceof DuplicateToolNameError) {\n    console.error('Conflicting tool name:', err.toolName)\n    // Fix: set a unique prefix on one of the clients\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a unique "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prefix"},{"type":"text","value":" on each client to avoid collisions — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":" does this automatically using the config key."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"lazy-tool-discovery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy Tool Discovery"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ lazy: true }"},{"type":"text","value":" to defer sending tool schemas to the LLM until it explicitly asks for them. This reduces token usage when working with tool-heavy servers."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools({ lazy: true })\n// All tools are marked lazy: true","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Works with the pool too:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\nconst tools = await pool.tools({ lazy: true })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./lazy-tool-discovery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy Tool Discovery"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for how the LLM discovers lazy tools at runtime."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-mcp-with-chat","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using MCP with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Quick Start above hands tools to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" manually via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools: await mcp.tools()"},{"type":"text","value":" and closes the client yourself. Two follow-on guides cover richer integrations:"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" own discovery and lifecycle."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Pass live clients and pools to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" option and it discovers tools and closes connections for you — no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"try/finally"},{"type":"text","value":" per route. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-managed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed MCP with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resources, prompts, and fully-typed manual tools."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Inject MCP resources and prompts into a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" run, cancel in-flight MCP calls, and spread "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":"-typed tools. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-manual","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"error-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Reference"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error class"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When thrown"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPConnectionError"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" fails to connect, or a method is called after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DuplicateToolNameError"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two tools have the same name within one client or across the pool"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPToolNotFoundError"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":" name passed to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools([...defs])"},{"type":"text","value":" is not found on the server"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPTaskRequiredToolError"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A task-required tool was bound via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools([...defs])"},{"type":"text","value":" or called via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"callTool()"},{"type":"text","value":" but the server does not declare the tasks capability for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools/call"},{"type":"text","value":", so the call could never execute"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MCPDuplicateToolNameError"},{"type":"text","value":" thrown when merging tools from multiple sources inside a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ mcp })"},{"type":"text","value":" run, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./mcp-managed#tool-name-collisions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed MCP with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"server-mcp-and-webmcp","text":"Server MCP and WebMCP","level":2},{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"quick-start","text":"Quick Start","level":2},{"id":"transports","text":"Transports","level":2},{"id":"http-streamable-http","text":"HTTP (Streamable HTTP)","level":3},{"id":"sse-server-sent-events","text":"SSE (Server-Sent Events)","level":3},{"id":"stdio-node-js-only","text":"stdio (Node.js only)","level":3},{"id":"custom-transport-escape-hatch","text":"Custom transport (escape hatch)","level":3},{"id":"authentication","text":"Authentication","level":2},{"id":"static-tokens-headers","text":"Static tokens (headers)","level":3},{"id":"oauth-authprovider","text":"OAuth (authProvider)","level":3},{"id":"three-modes-of-type-safety","text":"Three Modes of Type Safety","level":2},{"id":"mode-1-auto-discovery-client-tools","text":"Mode 1 — Auto-discovery (client.tools())","level":3},{"id":"mode-2-explicit-definitions-client-tools-defs","text":"Mode 2 — Explicit definitions (client.tools([...defs]))","level":3},{"id":"mode-3-generated-types-createmcpclientgeneratedserver","text":"Mode 3 — Generated types (createMCPClient<GeneratedServer>)","level":3},{"id":"tool-titles-annotations","text":"Tool Titles & Annotations","level":2},{"id":"limit-and-gate-tools","text":"Limit and Gate Tools","level":2},{"id":"multi-server-pool","text":"Multi-Server Pool","level":2},{"id":"per-server-access","text":"Per-server access","level":3},{"id":"disable-or-override-the-prefix","text":"Disable or override the prefix","level":3},{"id":"closing-the-pool","text":"Closing the pool","level":3},{"id":"lifecycle","text":"Lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"streaming-route-handlers-close-via-middleware","text":"Streaming route handlers — close via middleware","level":3},{"id":"manual-close-when-you-consume-the-stream-in-scope","text":"Manual close — when you consume the stream in scope","level":3},{"id":"await-using-explicit-resource-management","text":"await using (Explicit Resource Management)","level":3},{"id":"tool-name-collisions","text":"Tool Name Collisions","level":2},{"id":"lazy-tool-discovery","text":"Lazy Tool Discovery","level":2},{"id":"using-mcp-with-chat","text":"Using MCP with chat()","level":2},{"id":"error-reference","text":"Error Reference","level":2}]},"frameworks":["react","vue","solid","svelte","preact","angular","vanilla","octane"],"downloadMarkdown":"\n`@tanstack/ai-mcp` is a host-side [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) client for TanStack AI. It connects your server route to any MCP-compliant server and makes that server's tools, resources, and prompts available inside `chat()`.\n\n> MCP tool execution is **server-side only**. The `createMCPClient` call lives in a server route (or serverless function) — never in browser code.\n\n## Server MCP and WebMCP\n\nServer MCP and WebMCP solve different problems.\n\n| Integration | Where it runs | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Server MCP | Your server route | Connects TanStack `chat()` to tools, resources, and prompts from an MCP server. |\n| WebMCP | The browser page | Exposes executable client tools to a browser agent through `document.modelContext`. |\n\nWebMCP calls return directly to the browser agent. They do not become tool results in a TanStack chat run.\n\nSee [WebMCP Tools](./webmcp) to expose browser actions. If your server needs MCP server tools, continue with this guide.\n\n## Installation\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nvue: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nsolid: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\npreact: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nangular: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\noctane: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n> `createMCPClient` tries protocol `2026-07-28` first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake.\n>\n> Package names for an `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` import are in [MCP SDK packages](../migration/mcp-sdk).\n\n## Quick Start\n\nThe simplest integration is the managed `mcp` option: hand the client to `chat()` and it discovers the tools and closes the connection when the run ends — no lifecycle code at all.\n\n```ts ignore\n// src/routes/api.chat.ts  (TanStack Start)\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        const mcp = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: {\n            type: 'http',\n            url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n          },\n        })\n\n        // chat() discovers the tools and closes the client when the run ends.\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: { clients: [mcp] },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> Need fully-typed tool arguments, resources, prompts, or your own lifecycle? Spread tools manually instead — see [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual) and the [Lifecycle](#lifecycle) section below.\n\nOn the client side, consume the stream with `useChat` exactly as you would any other TanStack AI endpoint:\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/Chat.tsx\nimport { useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, status } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => (\n        <div key={m.id}>\n          <strong>{m.role}:</strong>{' '}\n          {m.parts.find((p) => p.type === 'text')?.content}\n        </div>\n      ))}\n      <button\n        onClick={() => sendMessage({ content: 'Hello' })}\n        disabled={status === 'streaming'}\n      >\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Transports\n\n### HTTP (Streamable HTTP)\n\nThe preferred transport for remote servers. Uses the MCP Streamable HTTP protocol.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### SSE (Server-Sent Events)\n\nFor servers that implement the legacy SSE transport.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'sse',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/sse',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### stdio (Node.js only)\n\nFor spawning a local MCP process. Because stdio imports Node-native modules, it is isolated behind a subpath import so edge bundles stay clean.\n\n```ts\nimport { stdioTransport } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp/stdio'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: stdioTransport({\n    command: 'node',\n    args: ['./my-mcp-server.js'],\n    env: { API_KEY: process.env.API_KEY ?? '' },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n### Custom transport (escape hatch)\n\nPass any `Transport` instance directly as the `transport` option. For in-process testing, `InMemoryTransport` is re-exported from `@tanstack/ai-mcp`:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient, InMemoryTransport } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst [clientTransport, serverTransport] = InMemoryTransport.createLinkedPair()\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: clientTransport })\n```\n\nFor a custom network transport, pass a `Transport` from `@modelcontextprotocol/client`:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/client'\n\nconst transport = new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(new URL('https://example.com/mcp'))\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport })\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\n### Static tokens (headers)\n\nFor servers that take a pre-provisioned API key or bearer token, pass `headers` on the `http`/`sse` transport config — they are sent with every request:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### OAuth (`authProvider`)\n\nIf the server implements the [MCP authorization spec](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/authorization) (OAuth 2.1), pass an `authProvider` on the `http` or `sse` config.\n\nThe `authProvider` value is an `OAuthClientProvider` from `@modelcontextprotocol/client`.\n\nThe transport attaches the token. The transport refreshes the token. The transport retries the request on 401.\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\nimport type { OAuthClientProvider } from '@modelcontextprotocol/client'\nimport { myTokenStore } from './token-store'\n\n// Server-side: back the provider with tokens you persist (database, KV, ...).\n// `tokens()` returning a valid (or refreshable) token set is all the SDK\n// needs to authenticate requests.\nconst myOAuthProvider: OAuthClientProvider = myTokenStore.provider()\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    authProvider: myOAuthProvider,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> **Interactive authorization (redirect flows).** Completing an authorization-code\n> grant requires calling `finishAuth(code)` on the transport after the user is\n> redirected back — and `createMCPClient` constructs the transport internally,\n> so it cannot expose it. If you need the interactive flow, build the transport\n> yourself and pass it in (the escape hatch above): construct a\n> `StreamableHTTPClientTransport` with your `authProvider`, keep a reference,\n> call `transport.finishAuth(code)` in your OAuth callback route, then hand the\n> transport to `createMCPClient({ transport })`. For typical server-side use —\n> a provider backed by pre-provisioned or stored tokens with working refresh —\n> the config form shown above is all you need.\n\n## Three Modes of Type Safety\n\n### Mode 1 — Auto-discovery (`client.tools()`)\n\nCall `tools()` with no arguments to discover every tool the server exposes. This requires no extra setup. Tool argument types are `unknown` at compile time; the MCP JSON Schema is used for runtime validation.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools()\n// tools: ServerTool[]  — args typed unknown at compile time\n```\n\nMCP schemas often use `$ref` or `oneOf`. On adapters built on the OpenAI API, those tools are sent without strict mode, and the adapter warns in development. See [Tools that cannot use strict mode](../adapters/openai.md#tools-that-cannot-use-strict-mode).\n\n> **Task-based tools are supported.** Tools that declare\n> `execution.taskSupport: 'required'` automatically run through the MCP SDK's\n> experimental `tasks/callToolStream` flow. TanStack AI waits through task\n> status updates and returns the terminal result to the model. Tools declaring\n> `taskSupport: 'optional'` continue to use ordinary `callTool` execution.\n> Task execution needs the server to declare the tasks capability for\n> `tools/call`; a server that lists a task-required tool without it is\n> skipped by auto-discovery (the tool could never be invoked).\n>\n> If the chat run aborts, TanStack AI stops waiting for the task and sends a\n> best-effort `tasks/cancel` for a remote task the server has already created.\n\n### Mode 2 — Explicit definitions (`client.tools([...defs])`)\n\nPass TanStack `toolDefinition()` instances to get full TypeScript types and Zod validation. Only the named tools are returned (allowlist). `MCPToolNotFoundError` is thrown if a name isn't on the server. Task-required tools use the same automatic task execution described in Mode 1.\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst searchDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'search',\n  description: 'Search for items',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ id: z.string(), title: z.string() })),\n})\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools([searchDef])\n// tools[0].execute is typed: (args: { query: string }) => ...\n```\n\n### Mode 3 — Generated types (`createMCPClient<GeneratedServer>`)\n\nRun the CLI against a live server to generate per-server `interface` types, then pass the generated type as a generic — tool names are narrowed to the server's literal names and pool config keys are compile-checked, with zero runtime overhead. (Tool *arguments* stay untyped on the discovery path — combine with Mode 2 for typed args.)\n\n> See [MCP Type Generation](./mcp-codegen) for the full `mcp.config.ts` setup, the `generate` CLI, and how to wire the generated types into `createMCPClient` and `createMCPClients`.\n\n## Tool Titles & Annotations\n\nMCP servers can ship display and behavior metadata alongside each tool: a human-readable `title` and a set of `annotations` hints (`readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `idempotentHint`, `openWorldHint`, plus a legacy `annotations.title`). `@tanstack/ai-mcp` forwards all of it onto each discovered tool's `metadata.mcp`, on **both** the auto-discovery and explicit-definition paths, so you can label tools in your UI and decide which ones need a confirmation step.\n\n| `metadata.mcp` field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `title` | `string` — display name, resolved with the spec's precedence: the tool's `title` → `annotations.title` → `name`. Always set. |\n| `annotations` | The server's `annotations` object, forwarded verbatim. Absent when the server declares none. |\n| `serverToolName` | `string` — server-native (unprefixed) tool name. |\n| `serverId` | The client's `prefix` (undefined when there is none). |\n| `uiResourceUri` | [MCP Apps](../mcp/apps) widget link, when the tool declares one. |\n\nThe block is typed, so just read it. `tools()` returns `McpServerTool`s — a plain `ServerTool` (it still drops straight into `chat({ tools })`) whose `metadata.mcp` is statically known to be present and shaped like the table above. No annotation, no cast, and a misspelled field is a compile error:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n\n// Trust comes from YOUR configuration — an allowlist of servers you operate or\n// have vetted — never from anything the server itself sends.\nconst trustedServers = new Set(['https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'])\nconst serverIsTrusted = trustedServers.has(url)\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\n\nconst tools = (await mcp.tools()).map((tool) => {\n  const meta = tool.metadata.mcp\n  const advertisedReadOnly = meta.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true\n  return {\n    ...tool,\n    // Approval is the default. A hint may only relax it for a server whose\n    // trust you established independently; on any other server the same hint\n    // is a label/recommendation and changes nothing about approval.\n    needsApproval: !(serverIsTrusted && advertisedReadOnly),\n  }\n})\n```\n\n> **Annotations are advisory, never a security boundary.** The MCP spec is explicit that every field — including `title` — is a hint that may not faithfully describe what the tool actually does, and a malicious or compromised server can claim anything (`readOnlyHint: true` on a tool that deletes records). Do not use them as the security boundary for an untrusted server: never let a hint alone waive approval, sandboxing, or authorization. On a server you have independently established as trusted, a hint may *relax* a confirmation step, as above; everywhere else, treat annotations as display labels and recommendations only — surface `readOnlyHint` as a badge (see the UI example below) rather than acting on it.\n\nTitles are display-only: they never change the tool `name` sent to the model, and a `prefix` still applies to the name (`wx_get_weather`), not to the title.\n\n`McpToolMetadata` and `McpServerTool` are both exported if you need to name the shapes in your own signatures (`ToolAnnotations` too, re-exported from the MCP SDK). You don't need them just to read the block.\n\nTo label tools in your UI, expose the forwarded metadata from a server route — the MCP client itself must stay server-side:\n\n```ts ignore\n// src/routes/api.mcp-tools.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/mcp-tools')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async () => {\n        await using mcp = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.MCP_URL! },\n        })\n        const catalog = (await mcp.tools()).map((tool) => ({\n          name: tool.name,\n          // `title` is always set — the fallback chain already ran.\n          title: tool.metadata.mcp.title,\n          description: tool.description,\n          readOnly: tool.metadata.mcp.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n        }))\n        return Response.json({ tools: catalog })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/ToolCatalog.tsx\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\n\ninterface ToolSummary {\n  name: string\n  title: string\n  description?: string\n  readOnly: boolean\n}\n\nexport function ToolCatalog() {\n  const [tools, setTools] = useState<Array<ToolSummary>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    fetch('/api/mcp-tools')\n      .then((res) => res.json())\n      .then((body: { tools: Array<ToolSummary> }) => setTools(body.tools))\n  }, [])\n\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {tools.map((tool) => (\n        <li key={tool.name}>\n          {/* Server-declared title, with the hint driving the badge */}\n          <strong>{tool.title}</strong> {tool.readOnly ? '(read-only)' : '(writes)'}\n          <div>{tool.description}</div>\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Limit and Gate Tools\n\nBy default, the model gets every tool that the server lists, and each tool runs without approval. A server can expose tools that write or delete data. Two client options control this:\n\n- `toolFilter`: return `false` to hide a tool from the model.\n- `needsApproval`: return `true` to pause the run for [approval](./tool-approval) before each call to the tool.\n\nBoth options receive the tool definition that the server sent. The definition has the native (unprefixed) `name`, the `title`, and the `annotations`. Both options are off by default.\n\nSend only the tools that the server marks read-only:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n})\n\nconst tools = await mcp.tools() // only read-only tools\n```\n\nA tool with no `annotations` does not pass this filter.\n\nOn a server that you do not trust, filter by name. You control the names, and the server controls the hints:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst allowed = new Set(['search_issues', 'get_issue'])\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => allowed.has(tool.name),\n})\n```\n\nKeep every tool, but ask the user before a tool runs:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n// Trust comes from your configuration, not from the server.\nconst trustedServers = new Set(['https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'])\nconst serverIsTrusted = trustedServers.has(url)\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url },\n  // A read-only hint skips approval only on a server that you trust.\n  needsApproval: (tool) =>\n    !(serverIsTrusted && tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true),\n})\n```\n\nThe run stops with an approval interrupt for each gated call. The client approves or denies it the same way as for any other tool. See [Tool Approval Flow](./tool-approval).\n\nIn a [pool](#multi-server-pool), each server gets its own options:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n    toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n  },\n  docs: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.DOCS_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n```\n\nThe options apply in these places:\n\n- **`tools()`**: `toolFilter` hides tools, and `needsApproval` marks tools.\n- **`tools([...defs])`**: `toolFilter` applies. A definition that the filter hides throws `MCPToolNotFoundError`. `needsApproval` does not apply, because each `toolDefinition` has its own `needsApproval`.\n- **`chat({ mcp })`**: both apply, because `chat()` calls `tools()` on the client. See [Managed MCP](./mcp-managed).\n- **[MCP Apps](../mcp/apps) widget calls**: both apply. A widget cannot call a tool that the filter hides. A widget call has no approval step, so a call to a tool that `needsApproval` marks returns `{ ok: false }`.\n- **`callTool()`**: neither applies. Your code calls the tool directly, and the model is not involved.\n\n> **The hints come from the server.** A compromised server can mark a delete tool `readOnlyHint: true`. Use a name allowlist or `needsApproval` for a server that you do not trust.\n\n`McpTool` is exported if you need to name the type of the `tool` argument.\n\n## Multi-Server Pool\n\n`createMCPClients` connects to many servers in parallel and merges their tools into one flat array. Each server's tools are automatically prefixed with the config key to prevent name collisions.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\n// tools: [github_search_repos, github_create_issue, linear_create_issue, ...]\nconst tools = await pool.tools()\n```\n\n`pool.tools()` collects all servers' tools and throws `DuplicateToolNameError` if any two names collide after prefixing.\n\n### Per-server access\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\nconst linearTools = await pool.clients.linear!.tools()\nconst resources = await pool.clients.github!.resources()\n```\n\n### Disable or override the prefix\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n    prefix: 'gh',           // override: \"gh_search_repos\"\n  },\n  internal: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.INTERNAL_MCP_URL! },\n    prefix: '',             // disable prefix entirely\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Closing the pool\n\n```ts ignore\nawait pool.close()\n// or\nawait using pool = await createMCPClients({ /* server configs */ })\n```\n\nIf any server fails to connect, already-connected clients are closed before the error is thrown — no leaks.\n\n## Lifecycle\n\n> **You can skip this entire section** by passing clients to `chat()` via the `mcp` option (as in the Quick Start) — `chat()` discovers tools and closes the connections when the run ends. See [Managed MCP with `chat()`](./mcp-managed). Read on only if you spread tools manually and own `close()` yourself.\n\nWhen you manage the client manually, it is **caller-owned**: `chat()` never closes it.\n\nTools execute **lazily while the response stream is consumed**, so only close the client after the stream is fully drained. In a route handler that returns a streaming `Response`, a `try/finally` around the `return` (or `await using` at function scope) closes the client *before* the body streams — in-flight tool calls would fail. Close in a middleware terminal hook instead.\n\n### Streaming route handlers — close via middleware\n\nExactly one of `onFinish`/`onAbort`/`onError` fires per run, after the agent loop ends:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n  const url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n  const mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: await mcp.tools(),\n    middleware: [\n      {\n        name: 'mcp-close',\n        onFinish: () => mcp.close(),\n        onAbort: () => mcp.close(),\n        onError: () => mcp.close(),\n      },\n    ],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n### Manual close — when you consume the stream in scope\n\n`try/finally` is correct when the stream is drained before the scope exits:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\ntry {\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: await mcp.tools(),\n  })\n  for await (const chunk of stream) {\n    // handle chunks — the stream is fully consumed inside this block\n  }\n} finally {\n  await mcp.close()\n}\n```\n\n### `await using` (Explicit Resource Management)\n\nIf your runtime supports `Symbol.asyncDispose` (Node 18.2+ with TypeScript `target: \"es2022\"` + `lib: [\"esnext\"]`), the same in-scope-consumption rule applies — the client closes when the block exits:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\nawait using mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools: await mcp.tools(),\n})\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  // handle chunks\n}\n// mcp.close() is called automatically when the block exits\n```\n\n## Tool Name Collisions\n\nWhen mixing tools from multiple sources, duplicate names throw `DuplicateToolNameError`:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients, DuplicateToolNameError } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\ntry {\n  const tools = await pool.tools()\n} catch (err) {\n  if (err instanceof DuplicateToolNameError) {\n    console.error('Conflicting tool name:', err.toolName)\n    // Fix: set a unique prefix on one of the clients\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse a unique `prefix` on each client to avoid collisions — `createMCPClients` does this automatically using the config key.\n\n## Lazy Tool Discovery\n\nPass `{ lazy: true }` to defer sending tool schemas to the LLM until it explicitly asks for them. This reduces token usage when working with tool-heavy servers.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools({ lazy: true })\n// All tools are marked lazy: true\n```\n\nWorks with the pool too:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\nconst tools = await pool.tools({ lazy: true })\n```\n\nSee [Lazy Tool Discovery](./lazy-tool-discovery) for how the LLM discovers lazy tools at runtime.\n\n## Using MCP with `chat()`\n\nThe Quick Start above hands tools to `chat()` manually via `tools: await mcp.tools()` and closes the client yourself. Two follow-on guides cover richer integrations:\n\n> **Let `chat()` own discovery and lifecycle.** Pass live clients and pools to `chat()` via the `mcp` option and it discovers tools and closes connections for you — no `try/finally` per route. See [Managed MCP with `chat()`](./mcp-managed).\n\n> **Resources, prompts, and fully-typed manual tools.** Inject MCP resources and prompts into a `chat()` run, cancel in-flight MCP calls, and spread `toolDefinition`-typed tools. See [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual).\n\n## Error Reference\n\n| Error class | When thrown |\n|---|---|\n| `MCPConnectionError` | `createMCPClient` fails to connect, or a method is called after `close()` |\n| `DuplicateToolNameError` | Two tools have the same name within one client or across the pool |\n| `MCPToolNotFoundError` | A `toolDefinition` name passed to `tools([...defs])` is not found on the server |\n| `MCPTaskRequiredToolError` | A task-required tool was bound via `tools([...defs])` or called via `callTool()` but the server does not declare the tasks capability for `tools/call`, so the call could never execute |\n\nFor the `MCPDuplicateToolNameError` thrown when merging tools from multiple sources inside a `chat({ mcp })` run, see [Managed MCP with `chat()`](./mcp-managed#tool-name-collisions).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: MCP Server Tools\nid: mcp\norder: 8\ndescription: \"Connect TanStack AI to any Model Context Protocol server with createMCPClient to discover and execute its tools.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - mcp\n  - model context protocol\n  - mcp tools\n  - mcp client\n  - server tools\n  - createMCPClient\n  - createMCPClients\n  - type safety\n---\n\n`@tanstack/ai-mcp` is a host-side [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) client for TanStack AI. It connects your server route to any MCP-compliant server and makes that server's tools, resources, and prompts available inside `chat()`.\n\n> MCP tool execution is **server-side only**. The `createMCPClient` call lives in a server route (or serverless function) — never in browser code.\n\n## Server MCP and WebMCP\n\nServer MCP and WebMCP solve different problems.\n\n| Integration | Where it runs | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Server MCP | Your server route | Connects TanStack `chat()` to tools, resources, and prompts from an MCP server. |\n| WebMCP | The browser page | Exposes executable client tools to a browser agent through `document.modelContext`. |\n\nWebMCP calls return directly to the browser agent. They do not become tool results in a TanStack chat run.\n\nSee [WebMCP Tools](./webmcp) to expose browser actions. If your server needs MCP server tools, continue with this guide.\n\n## Installation\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nvue: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nsolid: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\npreact: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nangular: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\noctane: @tanstack/ai-mcp @modelcontextprotocol/client\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n> `createMCPClient` tries protocol `2026-07-28` first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake.\n>\n> Package names for an `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` import are in [MCP SDK packages](../migration/mcp-sdk).\n\n## Quick Start\n\nThe simplest integration is the managed `mcp` option: hand the client to `chat()` and it discovers the tools and closes the connection when the run ends — no lifecycle code at all.\n\n```ts ignore\n// src/routes/api.chat.ts  (TanStack Start)\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/chat')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      POST: async ({ request }) => {\n        const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n        const mcp = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: {\n            type: 'http',\n            url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n          },\n        })\n\n        // chat() discovers the tools and closes the client when the run ends.\n        const stream = chat({\n          adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n          messages,\n          mcp: { clients: [mcp] },\n        })\n\n        return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> Need fully-typed tool arguments, resources, prompts, or your own lifecycle? Spread tools manually instead — see [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual) and the [Lifecycle](#lifecycle) section below.\n\nOn the client side, consume the stream with `useChat` exactly as you would any other TanStack AI endpoint:\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/Chat.tsx\nimport { useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, status } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((m) => (\n        <div key={m.id}>\n          <strong>{m.role}:</strong>{' '}\n          {m.parts.find((p) => p.type === 'text')?.content}\n        </div>\n      ))}\n      <button\n        onClick={() => sendMessage({ content: 'Hello' })}\n        disabled={status === 'streaming'}\n      >\n        Send\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Transports\n\n### HTTP (Streamable HTTP)\n\nThe preferred transport for remote servers. Uses the MCP Streamable HTTP protocol.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### SSE (Server-Sent Events)\n\nFor servers that implement the legacy SSE transport.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'sse',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/sse',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### stdio (Node.js only)\n\nFor spawning a local MCP process. Because stdio imports Node-native modules, it is isolated behind a subpath import so edge bundles stay clean.\n\n```ts\nimport { stdioTransport } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp/stdio'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: stdioTransport({\n    command: 'node',\n    args: ['./my-mcp-server.js'],\n    env: { API_KEY: process.env.API_KEY ?? '' },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\n### Custom transport (escape hatch)\n\nPass any `Transport` instance directly as the `transport` option. For in-process testing, `InMemoryTransport` is re-exported from `@tanstack/ai-mcp`:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient, InMemoryTransport } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst [clientTransport, serverTransport] = InMemoryTransport.createLinkedPair()\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: clientTransport })\n```\n\nFor a custom network transport, pass a `Transport` from `@modelcontextprotocol/client`:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/client'\n\nconst transport = new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(new URL('https://example.com/mcp'))\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport })\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\n### Static tokens (headers)\n\nFor servers that take a pre-provisioned API key or bearer token, pass `headers` on the `http`/`sse` transport config — they are sent with every request:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}` },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### OAuth (`authProvider`)\n\nIf the server implements the [MCP authorization spec](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/authorization) (OAuth 2.1), pass an `authProvider` on the `http` or `sse` config.\n\nThe `authProvider` value is an `OAuthClientProvider` from `@modelcontextprotocol/client`.\n\nThe transport attaches the token. The transport refreshes the token. The transport retries the request on 401.\n\n```ts ignore\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\nimport type { OAuthClientProvider } from '@modelcontextprotocol/client'\nimport { myTokenStore } from './token-store'\n\n// Server-side: back the provider with tokens you persist (database, KV, ...).\n// `tokens()` returning a valid (or refreshable) token set is all the SDK\n// needs to authenticate requests.\nconst myOAuthProvider: OAuthClientProvider = myTokenStore.provider()\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: {\n    type: 'http',\n    url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp',\n    authProvider: myOAuthProvider,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> **Interactive authorization (redirect flows).** Completing an authorization-code\n> grant requires calling `finishAuth(code)` on the transport after the user is\n> redirected back — and `createMCPClient` constructs the transport internally,\n> so it cannot expose it. If you need the interactive flow, build the transport\n> yourself and pass it in (the escape hatch above): construct a\n> `StreamableHTTPClientTransport` with your `authProvider`, keep a reference,\n> call `transport.finishAuth(code)` in your OAuth callback route, then hand the\n> transport to `createMCPClient({ transport })`. For typical server-side use —\n> a provider backed by pre-provisioned or stored tokens with working refresh —\n> the config form shown above is all you need.\n\n## Three Modes of Type Safety\n\n### Mode 1 — Auto-discovery (`client.tools()`)\n\nCall `tools()` with no arguments to discover every tool the server exposes. This requires no extra setup. Tool argument types are `unknown` at compile time; the MCP JSON Schema is used for runtime validation.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools()\n// tools: ServerTool[]  — args typed unknown at compile time\n```\n\nMCP schemas often use `$ref` or `oneOf`. On adapters built on the OpenAI API, those tools are sent without strict mode, and the adapter warns in development. See [Tools that cannot use strict mode](../adapters/openai.md#tools-that-cannot-use-strict-mode).\n\n> **Task-based tools are supported.** Tools that declare\n> `execution.taskSupport: 'required'` automatically run through the MCP SDK's\n> experimental `tasks/callToolStream` flow. TanStack AI waits through task\n> status updates and returns the terminal result to the model. Tools declaring\n> `taskSupport: 'optional'` continue to use ordinary `callTool` execution.\n> Task execution needs the server to declare the tasks capability for\n> `tools/call`; a server that lists a task-required tool without it is\n> skipped by auto-discovery (the tool could never be invoked).\n>\n> If the chat run aborts, TanStack AI stops waiting for the task and sends a\n> best-effort `tasks/cancel` for a remote task the server has already created.\n\n### Mode 2 — Explicit definitions (`client.tools([...defs])`)\n\nPass TanStack `toolDefinition()` instances to get full TypeScript types and Zod validation. Only the named tools are returned (allowlist). `MCPToolNotFoundError` is thrown if a name isn't on the server. Task-required tools use the same automatic task execution described in Mode 1.\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst searchDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'search',\n  description: 'Search for items',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ id: z.string(), title: z.string() })),\n})\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools([searchDef])\n// tools[0].execute is typed: (args: { query: string }) => ...\n```\n\n### Mode 3 — Generated types (`createMCPClient<GeneratedServer>`)\n\nRun the CLI against a live server to generate per-server `interface` types, then pass the generated type as a generic — tool names are narrowed to the server's literal names and pool config keys are compile-checked, with zero runtime overhead. (Tool *arguments* stay untyped on the discovery path — combine with Mode 2 for typed args.)\n\n> See [MCP Type Generation](./mcp-codegen) for the full `mcp.config.ts` setup, the `generate` CLI, and how to wire the generated types into `createMCPClient` and `createMCPClients`.\n\n## Tool Titles & Annotations\n\nMCP servers can ship display and behavior metadata alongside each tool: a human-readable `title` and a set of `annotations` hints (`readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `idempotentHint`, `openWorldHint`, plus a legacy `annotations.title`). `@tanstack/ai-mcp` forwards all of it onto each discovered tool's `metadata.mcp`, on **both** the auto-discovery and explicit-definition paths, so you can label tools in your UI and decide which ones need a confirmation step.\n\n| `metadata.mcp` field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `title` | `string` — display name, resolved with the spec's precedence: the tool's `title` → `annotations.title` → `name`. Always set. |\n| `annotations` | The server's `annotations` object, forwarded verbatim. Absent when the server declares none. |\n| `serverToolName` | `string` — server-native (unprefixed) tool name. |\n| `serverId` | The client's `prefix` (undefined when there is none). |\n| `uiResourceUri` | [MCP Apps](../mcp/apps) widget link, when the tool declares one. |\n\nThe block is typed, so just read it. `tools()` returns `McpServerTool`s — a plain `ServerTool` (it still drops straight into `chat({ tools })`) whose `metadata.mcp` is statically known to be present and shaped like the table above. No annotation, no cast, and a misspelled field is a compile error:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n\n// Trust comes from YOUR configuration — an allowlist of servers you operate or\n// have vetted — never from anything the server itself sends.\nconst trustedServers = new Set(['https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'])\nconst serverIsTrusted = trustedServers.has(url)\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\n\nconst tools = (await mcp.tools()).map((tool) => {\n  const meta = tool.metadata.mcp\n  const advertisedReadOnly = meta.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true\n  return {\n    ...tool,\n    // Approval is the default. A hint may only relax it for a server whose\n    // trust you established independently; on any other server the same hint\n    // is a label/recommendation and changes nothing about approval.\n    needsApproval: !(serverIsTrusted && advertisedReadOnly),\n  }\n})\n```\n\n> **Annotations are advisory, never a security boundary.** The MCP spec is explicit that every field — including `title` — is a hint that may not faithfully describe what the tool actually does, and a malicious or compromised server can claim anything (`readOnlyHint: true` on a tool that deletes records). Do not use them as the security boundary for an untrusted server: never let a hint alone waive approval, sandboxing, or authorization. On a server you have independently established as trusted, a hint may *relax* a confirmation step, as above; everywhere else, treat annotations as display labels and recommendations only — surface `readOnlyHint` as a badge (see the UI example below) rather than acting on it.\n\nTitles are display-only: they never change the tool `name` sent to the model, and a `prefix` still applies to the name (`wx_get_weather`), not to the title.\n\n`McpToolMetadata` and `McpServerTool` are both exported if you need to name the shapes in your own signatures (`ToolAnnotations` too, re-exported from the MCP SDK). You don't need them just to read the block.\n\nTo label tools in your UI, expose the forwarded metadata from a server route — the MCP client itself must stay server-side:\n\n```ts ignore\n// src/routes/api.mcp-tools.ts\nimport { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport const Route = createFileRoute('/api/mcp-tools')({\n  server: {\n    handlers: {\n      GET: async () => {\n        await using mcp = await createMCPClient({\n          transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.MCP_URL! },\n        })\n        const catalog = (await mcp.tools()).map((tool) => ({\n          name: tool.name,\n          // `title` is always set — the fallback chain already ran.\n          title: tool.metadata.mcp.title,\n          description: tool.description,\n          readOnly: tool.metadata.mcp.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n        }))\n        return Response.json({ tools: catalog })\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/ToolCatalog.tsx\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react'\n\ninterface ToolSummary {\n  name: string\n  title: string\n  description?: string\n  readOnly: boolean\n}\n\nexport function ToolCatalog() {\n  const [tools, setTools] = useState<Array<ToolSummary>>([])\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    fetch('/api/mcp-tools')\n      .then((res) => res.json())\n      .then((body: { tools: Array<ToolSummary> }) => setTools(body.tools))\n  }, [])\n\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {tools.map((tool) => (\n        <li key={tool.name}>\n          {/* Server-declared title, with the hint driving the badge */}\n          <strong>{tool.title}</strong> {tool.readOnly ? '(read-only)' : '(writes)'}\n          <div>{tool.description}</div>\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Limit and Gate Tools\n\nBy default, the model gets every tool that the server lists, and each tool runs without approval. A server can expose tools that write or delete data. Two client options control this:\n\n- `toolFilter`: return `false` to hide a tool from the model.\n- `needsApproval`: return `true` to pause the run for [approval](./tool-approval) before each call to the tool.\n\nBoth options receive the tool definition that the server sent. The definition has the native (unprefixed) `name`, the `title`, and the `annotations`. Both options are off by default.\n\nSend only the tools that the server marks read-only:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n})\n\nconst tools = await mcp.tools() // only read-only tools\n```\n\nA tool with no `annotations` does not pass this filter.\n\nOn a server that you do not trust, filter by name. You control the names, and the server controls the hints:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst allowed = new Set(['search_issues', 'get_issue'])\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n  toolFilter: (tool) => allowed.has(tool.name),\n})\n```\n\nKeep every tool, but ask the user before a tool runs:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n// Trust comes from your configuration, not from the server.\nconst trustedServers = new Set(['https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'])\nconst serverIsTrusted = trustedServers.has(url)\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url },\n  // A read-only hint skips approval only on a server that you trust.\n  needsApproval: (tool) =>\n    !(serverIsTrusted && tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true),\n})\n```\n\nThe run stops with an approval interrupt for each gated call. The client approves or denies it the same way as for any other tool. See [Tool Approval Flow](./tool-approval).\n\nIn a [pool](#multi-server-pool), each server gets its own options:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n    toolFilter: (tool) => tool.annotations?.readOnlyHint === true,\n  },\n  docs: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.DOCS_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n```\n\nThe options apply in these places:\n\n- **`tools()`**: `toolFilter` hides tools, and `needsApproval` marks tools.\n- **`tools([...defs])`**: `toolFilter` applies. A definition that the filter hides throws `MCPToolNotFoundError`. `needsApproval` does not apply, because each `toolDefinition` has its own `needsApproval`.\n- **`chat({ mcp })`**: both apply, because `chat()` calls `tools()` on the client. See [Managed MCP](./mcp-managed).\n- **[MCP Apps](../mcp/apps) widget calls**: both apply. A widget cannot call a tool that the filter hides. A widget call has no approval step, so a call to a tool that `needsApproval` marks returns `{ ok: false }`.\n- **`callTool()`**: neither applies. Your code calls the tool directly, and the model is not involved.\n\n> **The hints come from the server.** A compromised server can mark a delete tool `readOnlyHint: true`. Use a name allowlist or `needsApproval` for a server that you do not trust.\n\n`McpTool` is exported if you need to name the type of the `tool` argument.\n\n## Multi-Server Pool\n\n`createMCPClients` connects to many servers in parallel and merges their tools into one flat array. Each server's tools are automatically prefixed with the config key to prevent name collisions.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\n// tools: [github_search_repos, github_create_issue, linear_create_issue, ...]\nconst tools = await pool.tools()\n```\n\n`pool.tools()` collects all servers' tools and throws `DuplicateToolNameError` if any two names collide after prefixing.\n\n### Per-server access\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\nconst linearTools = await pool.clients.linear!.tools()\nconst resources = await pool.clients.github!.resources()\n```\n\n### Disable or override the prefix\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! },\n    prefix: 'gh',           // override: \"gh_search_repos\"\n  },\n  internal: {\n    transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.INTERNAL_MCP_URL! },\n    prefix: '',             // disable prefix entirely\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Closing the pool\n\n```ts ignore\nawait pool.close()\n// or\nawait using pool = await createMCPClients({ /* server configs */ })\n```\n\nIf any server fails to connect, already-connected clients are closed before the error is thrown — no leaks.\n\n## Lifecycle\n\n> **You can skip this entire section** by passing clients to `chat()` via the `mcp` option (as in the Quick Start) — `chat()` discovers tools and closes the connections when the run ends. See [Managed MCP with `chat()`](./mcp-managed). Read on only if you spread tools manually and own `close()` yourself.\n\nWhen you manage the client manually, it is **caller-owned**: `chat()` never closes it.\n\nTools execute **lazily while the response stream is consumed**, so only close the client after the stream is fully drained. In a route handler that returns a streaming `Response`, a `try/finally` around the `return` (or `await using` at function scope) closes the client *before* the body streams — in-flight tool calls would fail. Close in a middleware terminal hook instead.\n\n### Streaming route handlers — close via middleware\n\nExactly one of `onFinish`/`onAbort`/`onError` fires per run, after the agent loop ends:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n  const url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\n  const mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: await mcp.tools(),\n    middleware: [\n      {\n        name: 'mcp-close',\n        onFinish: () => mcp.close(),\n        onAbort: () => mcp.close(),\n        onError: () => mcp.close(),\n      },\n    ],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n### Manual close — when you consume the stream in scope\n\n`try/finally` is correct when the stream is drained before the scope exits:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\ntry {\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: await mcp.tools(),\n  })\n  for await (const chunk of stream) {\n    // handle chunks — the stream is fully consumed inside this block\n  }\n} finally {\n  await mcp.close()\n}\n```\n\n### `await using` (Explicit Resource Management)\n\nIf your runtime supports `Symbol.asyncDispose` (Node 18.2+ with TypeScript `target: \"es2022\"` + `lib: [\"esnext\"]`), the same in-scope-consumption rule applies — the client closes when the block exits:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\nconst url = 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp'\nawait using mcp = await createMCPClient({ transport: { type: 'http', url } })\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools: await mcp.tools(),\n})\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  // handle chunks\n}\n// mcp.close() is called automatically when the block exits\n```\n\n## Tool Name Collisions\n\nWhen mixing tools from multiple sources, duplicate names throw `DuplicateToolNameError`:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients, DuplicateToolNameError } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\n\ntry {\n  const tools = await pool.tools()\n} catch (err) {\n  if (err instanceof DuplicateToolNameError) {\n    console.error('Conflicting tool name:', err.toolName)\n    // Fix: set a unique prefix on one of the clients\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUse a unique `prefix` on each client to avoid collisions — `createMCPClients` does this automatically using the config key.\n\n## Lazy Tool Discovery\n\nPass `{ lazy: true }` to defer sending tool schemas to the LLM until it explicitly asks for them. This reduces token usage when working with tool-heavy servers.\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\nconst tools = await mcp.tools({ lazy: true })\n// All tools are marked lazy: true\n```\n\nWorks with the pool too:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMCPClients } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst pool = await createMCPClients({\n  github: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.GITHUB_MCP_URL! } },\n  linear: { transport: { type: 'http', url: process.env.LINEAR_MCP_URL! } },\n})\nconst tools = await pool.tools({ lazy: true })\n```\n\nSee [Lazy Tool Discovery](./lazy-tool-discovery) for how the LLM discovers lazy tools at runtime.\n\n## Using MCP with `chat()`\n\nThe Quick Start above hands tools to `chat()` manually via `tools: await mcp.tools()` and closes the client yourself. Two follow-on guides cover richer integrations:\n\n> **Let `chat()` own discovery and lifecycle.** Pass live clients and pools to `chat()` via the `mcp` option and it discovers tools and closes connections for you — no `try/finally` per route. See [Managed MCP with `chat()`](./mcp-managed).\n\n> **Resources, prompts, and fully-typed manual tools.** Inject MCP resources and prompts into a `chat()` run, cancel in-flight MCP calls, and spread `toolDefinition`-typed tools. See [Manual MCP: typed tools, resources & prompts](./mcp-manual).\n\n## Error Reference\n\n| Error class | When thrown |\n|---|---|\n| `MCPConnectionError` | `createMCPClient` fails to connect, or a method is called after `close()` |\n| `DuplicateToolNameError` | Two tools have the same name within one client or across the pool |\n| `MCPToolNotFoundError` | A `toolDefinition` name passed to `tools([...defs])` is not found on the server |\n| `MCPTaskRequiredToolError` | A task-required tool was bound via `tools([...defs])` or called via `callTool()` but the server does not declare the tasks capability for `tools/call`, so the call could never execute |\n\nFor the `MCPDuplicateToolNameError` thrown when merging tools from multiple sources inside a `chat({ mcp })` run, see [Managed MCP with `chat()`](./mcp-managed#tool-name-collisions).\n"}
